# Part 4: Paley-7 against the Q resonator (NUMEROLOGY check)
   Paley tournament P7 (x -> x + {1,2,4}): eigenvalues [(-0.5-1.322876j), (-0.5-1.322876j), (-0.5-1.322876j), (-0.5+1.322876j), (-0.5+1.322876j), (-0.5+1.322876j), (3+0j)]
   minimal polynomial of (-1 + sqrt(-7))/2: lam**2 + lam + 2; the Q linearisation d(n) = -d(n-1)/2 - d(n-2)/2 has characteristic polynomial 2 z^2 + z + 1 with roots [-1/4 - sqrt(7)*I/4, -1/4 + sqrt(7)*I/4]
   reciprocal: 2 z^2 + z + 1 at z = 1/lambda for the roots lambda of lambda^2 + lambda + 2: [0, 0]
   doubly regular tournaments on n = 4t+3 vertices have eigenvalues (-1 +- i sqrt n)/2, roots of lambda^2 + lambda + (n+1)/4: the coincidence with the two-branch, weight-1/2, lags-1-and-2 polynomial needs (n+1)/4 = 2, i.e. n = 7 only.
   N((-1+sqrt(-7))/2) = 2: a prime above 2 in Q(sqrt(-7)) (2 splits since -7 = 1 mod 8). Ramanujan-Nagell 2^n - 7 = x^2, n <= 300: [3, 4, 5, 7, 15]

